15	+Feature: Account table access
16	+  User story:
17	+  * As a user of a plugin
18	+  * Logged in through a given space's anonymous account
19	+  * I want to see and keep up to date the accounts of the spaces I share
20	+  * And nobody else should see or change them without access to those spaces
21	+
22	+  Acceptance criteria:
23	+  * Partial access to a space shows its accounts
24	+  * Reader access also shows their identifiers and the space's account list
25	+  * Editors of a space can write the identifiers of its unowned accounts, and delete those accounts
26	+  * An account's owner can write its identifiers and delete it
27	+
28	+  Background:
29	+    Given the database is blank
30	+    And the user user1 opens the Roam plugin in space s1
31	+    And the user user2 opens the Roam plugin in space s2
32	+    And the user user3 opens the Roam plugin in space s3
33	+    And user of space s1 creates group g1
34	+    And user of space s1 adds space s2 to group g1
35	+    And the account of space s1 is called anon1
36	+    And the account of space s2 is called anon2
37	+
38	+  Scenario: Without shared access, a space sees only its own account rows
39	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should see these account table rows:
40	+      | table           | rows         |
41	+      | PlatformAccount | anon2, user2 |
42	+      | AgentIdentifier | user2        |
43	+      | LocalAccess     | user2@s2     |
44	+
45	+  Scenario: Partial access shows a space's accounts, not their identifiers
46	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
47	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
48	+      | g1           | s1        | partial     |
49	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should see these account table rows:
50	+      | table           | rows                |
51	+      | PlatformAccount | anon2, user1, user2 |
52	+      | AgentIdentifier | user2               |
53	+      | LocalAccess     | user2@s2            |
54	+
55	+  Scenario: Reader access also shows identifiers and the space's account list
56	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
57	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
58	+      | g1           | s1        | reader      |
59	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should see these account table rows:
60	+      | table           | rows                |
61	+      | PlatformAccount | anon2, user1, user2 |
62	+      | AgentIdentifier | user1, user2        |
63	+      | LocalAccess     | user1@s1, user2@s2  |
64	+
65	+  Scenario: Editors write the identifiers of their spaces' unowned accounts
66	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
67	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
68	+      | g1           | s1        | editor      |
69	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these identifier write results:
70	+      | operation | account | result  |
71	+      | insert    | user1   | allowed |
72	+      | update    | user1   | allowed |
73	+      | delete    | user1   | allowed |
74	+      | insert    | user2   | allowed |
75	+      | insert    | anon2   | allowed |
76	+      | insert    | user3   | denied  |
77	+      | update    | user3   | denied  |
78	+      | delete    | user3   | denied  |
79	+
80	+  Scenario: Readers cannot write identifiers
81	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
82	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
83	+      | g1           | s1        | reader      |
84	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these identifier write results:
85	+      | operation | account | result |
86	+      | insert    | user1   | denied |
87	+      | update    | user1   | denied |
88	+      | delete    | user1   | denied |
89	+
90	+  # An account with identifiers cannot be deleted by any client: the AgentIdentifier foreign key
91	+  # has no ON DELETE action. These accounts have none, so the policy decides.
92	+  Scenario: Editors delete their spaces' unowned accounts, and owners their own
93	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
94	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
95	+      | g1           | s1        | editor      |
96	+    And PlatformAccount are added to the database:
97	+      | $id     | name    | platform | account_local_id |
98	+      | author1 | author1 | Roam     | author1          |
99	+      | author3 | author3 | Roam     | author3          |
100	+    And LocalAccess are added to the database:
101	+      | _account_id | _space_id |
102	+      | author1     | s1        |
103	+      | author3     | s3        |
104	+      | anon1       | s1        |
105	+    Then a user logged in space s2 should get these account write results:
106	+      | operation | account | column | value | result  |
107	+      | delete    | anon1   |        |       | denied  |
108	+      | delete    | author3 |        |       | denied  |
109	+      | delete    | author1 |        |       | allowed |
110	+      | delete    | anon2   |        |       | allowed |
111	+
112	+  Scenario: Editors cannot write an account in their space that someone owns
113	+    When SpaceAccess are added to the database:
114	+      | _account_uid | _space_id | permissions |
115	+      | g1           | s1        | editor      |
